// D3 — checker-logic attack: site-id / module scale. Category (d).
//
// Attack: 5,000 distinct conditional sites, every site with a
// data-independent outcome (else always taken; acc never equals the
// compared constant). Tests the tracer/checker instrumentation map and
// event ingestion at module scale: fixed-size site tables, site-id
// encoding assumptions, and map/event-count reconciliation with
// thousands of distinct sites. The plant is genuinely dirty: after the
// sites, 8 tv_usec bytes are emitted, varying run to run.
// Pure Zag; no C shim.
//
// TOOLCHAIN CEILING (documented red-team finding): the past-u16
// (65,536+) site variant of this attack could not be BUILT with the
// pinned toolchain — compile cost is superlinear in branch-site count
// (5k sites = 62s; 20k sites > 6 min and climbing; 80k sites never
// finished across three attempts). M3's "full coverage" claim therefore
// extends over module scales the pinned toolchain cannot compile, i.e.
// the method is untestable exactly where site-id-width attacks live.
//
// Believed defeated: checker site-id width / map scale; rule 2 at scale.
fn sites(acc:i32)i32 {
    let a:i32=acc;
    if(a==(0-200000)){a=7;}else{a=a+1;}
    if(a==(1-200000)){a=7;}else{a=a+1;}
